                        "entry_id": "e4954d0e-d22a-3687-b179-9e010a78cc50",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A transliteration of the Russian surname Бре́жнев (Bréžnev); usually refers to the Russian revolutionary and Soviet Secretary of the Central Committee of the CPSU(b) Leonid Brezhnev."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "f4b60463-1db0-4db6-8667-1c88d57d02b8",
                                "sense_id": "dd375f20-d167-3237-9168-8ba4b1fb5a3d",
                                "text": "Victory Day was first celebrated in the Soviet Union to mark the 20th anniversary of the German defeat in 1965. What had until then been just another workday became a public holiday which, as Russian historian Kirill Kobrin argues, was used from the outset as a political tool, with Soviet leader Leonid Brezhnev keen to use the event as a means of boosting the USSR’s image internationally."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
